! Gypsum dissolution with a sulfur isotope, at depth — Omphalos/rhea example.
!
! A dilute water advects through gypsum sand and dissolves it. Sulfur is carried as
! two species, SO4-- and S34O4--, and neither the deck nor the shipped database
! contains the second one: Omphalos builds the whole S34 system before each run,
! from the config's database_isotopes block.
!
! isotope_pressure_series.yaml then sweeps the pressure the log K columns are
! computed at. The point of the example is what that does to the isotope pair.
! pyGCC can recompute CaSO4(aq) and has never heard of CaS34O4(aq), so a naive
! recomputation would move one and not the other -- and the gap between them is an
! equilibrium fractionation nobody asked for. Omphalos copies each parent's new
! column onto its copy, so the pair stays together and the isotope ratio the fluid
! carries is the ratio the solid has.

TITLE
Gypsum dissolution with S34, 1 to 1000 bar.
END

RUNTIME
time_units       years
timestep_max     0.05
timestep_init    1.e-10
time_tolerance   0.1
hindmarsh        true
correction_max   2.0
debye-huckel     true
database_sweep   no
speciate_only    false
gimrt            true
graphics         tecplot
database         datacom.dbs
screen_output    1000
END

! The pore velocity is q/phi = 100 m/yr, so the column flushes in about a year.
! Gypsum is abundant and dissolves slowly enough that the solid is barely touched
! over these times; the 5 yr slice is the one the analysis uses.

OUTPUT
time_units           years
spatial_profile      2 5 20
END

TRANSPORT
distance_units meters
time_units     years
fix_diffusion  0.0
dispersivity   0.0
END

BOUNDARY_CONDITIONS
x_begin   inflow    flux
x_end     initial   flux
END

INITIAL_CONDITIONS
initial 1-100
END

! Sulfur enters at close to its natural 34S abundance, and the two gypsums are
! present in that ratio -- 0.0132 against 0.3000, or 4.21%. They share a rate
! constant, a log K and a *specific* surface area, so their reactive areas follow
! the amount of each phase present and keep doing so as the column dissolves.
! Far from equilibrium that area ratio is what the fluid picks up. Cl- carries the
! charge balance, and there is no carbonate anywhere in this system, deliberately.

CONDITION inflow
units            mol/kg
temperature      25.0
H2O              55.50843506
pH               7.00
Ca++             1.0e-6
SO4--            9.58e-7
S34O4--          4.21e-8
Na+              0.001
Cl-              charge
Gypsum           0.3000   specific_surface_area  1.0
Gypsum34         0.0132   specific_surface_area  1.0
END

CONDITION initial
units            mol/kg
temperature      25.0
H2O              55.50843506
pH               7.00
Ca++             1.0e-6
SO4--            9.58e-7
S34O4--          4.21e-8
Na+              0.001
Cl-              charge
Gypsum           0.3000   specific_surface_area  1.0
Gypsum34         0.0132   specific_surface_area  1.0
END

TEMPERATURE
set_temperature  25.0
END

POROSITY
fix_porosity     0.30
END

PRIMARY_SPECIES
H2O
H+
Ca++
SO4--
S34O4--
Na+
Cl-
END

! CaSO4(aq) and HSO4- are the pair that matters: pyGCC recomputes them, so their
! S34 copies are the ones a recomputation would otherwise leave behind.

SECONDARY_SPECIES
OH-
CaSO4(aq)
CaS34O4(aq)
HSO4-
HS34O4-
CaOH+
NaSO4-
NaS34O4-
END

MINERALS
Gypsum     -label default   -rate -8.00
Gypsum34   -label default   -rate -8.00
END

! This block is what makes CrunchTope treat the S34 species as the rare partners of
! their S32 counterparts, rather than as unrelated components that happen to share a
! log K. It needs a line per pair -- for the mineral as well as the primary species.
!
! Without the mineral line, Gypsum and Gypsum34 are independent phases, each driving
! its own ion activity product to the same K, and the fluid degenerates to
! 34SO4 = SO4 regardless of how much of each mineral is present.

ISOTOPES
primary  S34O4--   SO4--    0.0441626
mineral  Gypsum34  Gypsum   none
END

DISCRETIZATION
xzones 100 1.0
END

FLOW
time_units       years
distance_units   meters
constant_flow    30.0
END
